        </div>
        <p>Suppose at some time {`\\(t\\)`}, the particle is at position Q on the path. After small time {`\\(dt\\)`}, the particle has moved to another point R on the path. Construct a right-angled triangle with QR as hypotenus. In the reference frame, if we write {`\\(Q = (x, y)\\)`}, then we write {`\\(R = (x + dx, y + dy)\\)`} denoting the change in {`\\(x\\)`} and {`\\(y\\)`} in time {`\\(dt\\)`}. The infinitesimal distance represented by {`\\(ds\\)`} as the length of the hypotenus will be given by {`\\[ds = \\sqrt{(dx)^2 + (dy)^2}=\\sqrt{1 + \\Big[\\frac{dy}{dx}\\Big]^2}dx\\]`}</p>
        <p>
            {`\\(ds\\)`} is approximately equal to the path segment QR. But in the limit {`\\(dt\\to 0\\)`}, approximation becomes exact equality. To get the total length, we need to integrate {`\\(ds\\)`}.
            {`\\[
                \\begin{eqnarray}
                s = \\int ds &=& \\int_{x_i}^{x_f}\\sqrt{1 + \\Big[\\frac{dy}{dx}\\Big]^2}dx\\\\
                             &=& \\int_{x_i}^{x_f}\\sqrt{1 + [f'(x)]^2}dx
                \\end{eqnarray}
                \\]`}
            where {`\\(f'(x)\\)`} denotes the derivative of {`\\(f(x)\\)`} with respect to {`\\(x\\)`}.
        </p>
